theory CompliantAttach
begin

/*
================================================================================
  SUPERSESSION, SESSIONS AND QUEUES -- what an honest serving node stops doing
================================================================================

  WHICH TREE THIS IS.  `compliant/`.  Every property here is about a node
  doing what it promised, and none of it is checkable by anyone else.
  `wire-only/attach.spthy` holds the verifiable half (endpoint authentication)
  and is explicit that it holds nothing more.

  WHAT IS BEING CHECKED.  Three obligations, written in three places, that had
  never been read against one another:

    design 12.6.5 -- "A party holding AUTHENTICATED SUPERSESSION EVIDENCE for
      a binding ... MUST NOT continue serving it: sessions under the
      superseded credential terminate, and nothing further is delivered to it,
      queued material and capture-key grants included."

    `infra-client-requirements.md` -- "Stop serving a binding you have
      verified superseded ... Fail-open is for staleness you cannot resolve,
      never for knowledge you hold."

    `light-client-requirements.md` -- "On a sibling, trust-bearing operations
      are unavailable."

  THE ONE SENTENCE THAT SHAPES THE MODEL.  design 12.6.5 says the rule
  "bites on SESSIONS AND QUEUES ALREADY RUNNING rather than on future
  lookups", because inside the horizon supersession is enforced by
  REPLACEMENT: the adoption overwrites the key, so a future lookup finds
  nothing superseded to serve.  A model that gated lookups would therefore be
  modelling the wrong thing.  What is modelled is the running state -- a live
  session and a queue with material in it -- because that is where a check is
  actually required.

  AND THE IGNORANCE CASE IS NOT A GAP.  "Outside the horizon there is no
  supersession to know, the adoption having never arrived -- those parties
  hold the old key until its attestation expires."  A node with no evidence
  that keeps serving is CORRECT, and `serving_without_evidence_is_reachable`
  asserts that trace survives.  Losing it would mean the model had started
  assuming supersession is global knowledge, which is the no-shared-state rule
  violated in the one place it is most tempting.
*/

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SETUP
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  THE CREDENTIAL IS THE CLIENT'S, not the session's, and this matters: several
  nodes serve one client under ONE binding -- a patron and the siblings that
  stand in for it (design 14.1.2).  Minting a fresh key per session would make
  each node's credential its own and quietly destroy the only interesting
  question here, which is what happens when supersession reaches one node and
  not another.
*/

rule Client_Credential:
    [ Fr(~k) ]
  --[ Credential($C, ~k) ]->
    [ !Cred($C, ~k) ]

/*  THE ROLE TRAVELS WITH THE SESSION, not with the (node, client) pair.  A
    node can hold a primary session for one client and stand in as a sibling
    for another, and design 14.1.2 makes the mode a property of the topology
    read AT ATTACH TIME.  Carrying it in the `Session` fact is what stops a
    node that is primary somewhere from borrowing that authority here.     */
/*  ONE SERVING STATE PER BINDING, and supersession consumes it.

    The first version gave each session its own `Session` fact, and all three
    obligations falsified: a node could open two sessions under one
    credential, supersession ended one, and the other kept serving.  That is
    not a defect in the design -- design 12.6.5 says "SESSIONS under the
    superseded credential terminate", plural, and the node's duty is to the
    BINDING rather than to whichever session happened to be consumed.

    So the serving capability is one fact per (node, client, credential).
    Every serving rule consumes and restores it; supersession consumes it and
    puts nothing back.  A rule cannot forget to check a fact it needs to fire,
    which is why this is stronger than a flag and is the modelling of
    "enforced by replacement rather than by a check".

    THE ROLE TRAVELS WITH IT, because design 14.1.2 fixes the mode from the
    node's topology read at attach time.  A node that is primary for one
    client and a stand-in for another cannot borrow the first role for the
    second.                                                                */
rule Attach_Primary:
    [ !Cred($C, k) ]
  --[ Opened($N, $C, k), ServingAs($N, $C, k, 'primary') ]->
    [ Serving($N, $C, k, 'primary') ]

rule Attach_Sibling:
    [ !Cred($C, k) ]
  --[ Opened($N, $C, k), ServingAs($N, $C, k, 'sibling') ]->
    [ Serving($N, $C, k, 'sibling') ]

restriction OneServingStatePerBinding:
  "All N C k #i #j. Opened(N, C, k) @ #i & Opened(N, C, k) @ #j ==> #i = #j"

// Material arrives for a client and waits.  design 14.1.4: the node holds
// ciphertext for an offline client.
rule Enqueue:
    [ Fr(~m) ]
  --[ Enqueued($N, $C, ~m) ]->
    [ Queued($N, $C, ~m) ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ORDINARY SERVICE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
*/

rule Deliver:
    [ Serving($N, $C, k, role), Queued($N, $C, m) ]
  --[ Delivered($N, $C, k, m), ServedUnder($N, $C, k), ServedAs($N, $C, k, role) ]->
    [ Serving($N, $C, k, role) ]

/*  A TRUST-BEARING operation -- a patron countersignature, the thing a
    sibling cannot do.  The guard is the serving state's own role, which is
    what `light-client-requirements.md` describes from the other end: "on a
    sibling, trust-bearing operations are unavailable".                     */
rule Trust_Bearing_Operation:
    [ Serving($N, $C, k, 'primary') ]
  --[ TrustBearing($N, $C, k), ServedUnder($N, $C, k), ServedAs($N, $C, k, 'primary') ]->
    [ Serving($N, $C, k, 'primary') ]

/*
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  SUPERSESSION ARRIVES
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The evidence is authenticated by the time it gets here -- design 12.6.5 says
  "a verified reissue chain ... a recovery it has validated", and validating it
  is `wire-only/recovery.spthy`'s subject rather than this theory's.  What is
  modelled here is the CONSEQUENCE.
*/

rule Supersession_Arrives:
    [ Serving($N, $C, k, role) ]
  --[ EvidenceHeld($N, $C, k), Terminated($N, $C, k) ]->
    [ ]

/*
================================================================================
  PROPERTIES
================================================================================
*/

/*  ============================================================
    NOT DISCHARGED UNBOUNDED -- but verified over a bounded model
    ============================================================

    THE CLAIMS, which are what this theory exists for:

      lemma nothing_served_under_a_credential_this_node_superseded:
        all-traces
        "All N C k #i #j.
            EvidenceHeld(N, C, k) @ #i & ServedUnder(N, C, k) @ #j
              ==> #j < #i"

      lemma nothing_delivered_after_supersession:
        all-traces
        "All N C k m #i #j.
            EvidenceHeld(N, C, k) @ #i & Delivered(N, C, k, m) @ #j
              ==> #j < #i"

    Both are left commented out because TAMARIN DOES NOT DISCHARGE THEM, and
    a lemma the gate never proves must not sit in the file looking proven.

    WHY, and it is the same cause as `compliant/currency.spthy`'s open
    obligation: `Deliver` and `Trust_Bearing_Operation` consume `Serving` and
    restore it, which is faithful -- serving does not end the binding -- but
    the backward search for the origin of `Serving($N,$C,k,role)` then
    regresses through unboundedly many prior services.  Neither lemma was
    FALSIFIED; there is no counterexample, only no proof inside 420 seconds.

    WHAT IS ESTABLISHED, and it is more than belief.  `attach.bounded`
    appends a bound -- at most one supersession, at most two services -- and
    over that finite space BOTH obligations verify, in 84 and 46 steps.
    `run-all.sh` builds that file by appending the fragment to THIS one, so
    the rules have a single source and the bounded result follows any change
    to them.  Keeping the bound in a fragment rather than in this file is also
    what preserves `no_trust_bearing_operation_on_a_sibling` as an UNBOUNDED
    result; carrying the restrictions here would have quietly demoted it.

    WHAT ELSE HOLDS HERE: the role gate, both anti-vacuity witnesses, and the
    ignorance witness, all unbounded.

    THE SAME CAUSE IN TWO THEORIES IS THE USEFUL PART.  Both obligations are
    "a node stops doing X once it knows Y", and both models express X as a
    capability the node consumes and restores.  Whatever encoding closes one
    will close the other, so this is one modelling problem rather than two.
    AND THEY ARE DISCHARGED ELSEWHERE.  `tla/SupersessionDiscipline.tla`
    checks both exhaustively over a finite instance, as safety properties of
    a mutable local state machine.  The Tamarin difficulty was isolated to a
    three-rule theory: delete the restore and the same lemma verifies in four
    steps -- a fact about the backward search, not about the claim.
    ============================================================  */

/*  A SOURCE INVARIANT, needed for the same reason `compliant/currency.spthy`
    needs one: the serving rules consume `Serving` and restore it, so a
    backward search for where the serving state came from regresses through
    unboundedly many prior services.  Naming the attach it came from closes
    the regress.                                                            */
lemma service_follows_an_attach [reuse, use_induction]:
  all-traces
  "All N C k role #j. ServedAs(N, C, k, role) @ #j
      ==> Ex #i. ServingAs(N, C, k, role) @ #i & #i < #j"

/*  THE ROLE GATE.  A session opened in failover performs no trust-bearing
    operation.                                                              */
lemma no_trust_bearing_operation_on_a_sibling:
  all-traces
  "All N C k #i #j.
      ServingAs(N, C, k, 'sibling') @ #i & TrustBearing(N, C, k) @ #j
        ==> F"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY, and the reason the obligations above are not satisfied by a
    node that simply never serves anyone.                                   */
lemma delivery_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C k m #i. Delivered(N, C, k, m) @ #i"

lemma trust_bearing_operation_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C k #i. TrustBearing(N, C, k) @ #i"

/*  THE IGNORANCE WITNESS.  design 12.6.5: "Outside the horizon there is no
    supersession to know ... those parties hold the old key until its
    attestation expires, which is the ignorance the table above is for and
    NOT A FAILURE."  A second node delivering under a credential the first has
    superseded is behaving correctly, and this keeps that trace reachable.
    If it ever falsifies, the model has acquired a global supersession fact
    and broken the no-shared-state rule in the one place most tempting.    */
lemma serving_without_evidence_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N1 N2 C k m #a #b.
      EvidenceHeld(N1, C, k) @ #a
    & Delivered(N2, C, k, m) @ #b
    & #a < #b
    & not(N1 = N2)"


/*
================================================================================
  0-RTT EARLY DATA -- what a conforming server defers
================================================================================

  `wire-format.md` Section 9.1: "A server MUST NOT process an `Attach`
  received in TLS 1.3 0-RTT early data -- reject it or defer it until
  handshake [completion] ... early data is replayable, and a replayed
  `Attach` re-binds session state ... THE RULE SITS ON THE SERVER BECAUSE
  THAT IS WHERE IT IS CHECKABLE."

  WHY IT IS IN THIS TREE.  That last clause places it: checkable BY THE
  SERVER, about its own processing.  No counterparty can tell whether a
  server deferred early data or acted on it, so this is a conformance
  obligation and not a wire property.

  REPLAYABILITY IS MODELLED AS PERSISTENCE.  `!EarlyData` is a persistent
  fact, so it can be consumed any number of times -- which is exactly what
  "early data is replayable" means and is the only property of 0-RTT this
  needs.  A conforming server consumes a LINEAR `Handshaken` token instead,
  so one completed handshake admits one binding.
*/

rule Client_Sends_Attach_Early:
    [ Fr(~a) ]
  --[ SentEarly($C, ~a) ]->
    [ !EarlyData($C, ~a) ]

rule Handshake_Completes:
    [ ]
  --[ HandshakeDone($N, $C) ]->
    [ Handshaken($N, $C) ]

/*  THE CONFORMING SERVER.  It has the early data -- it cannot un-receive it
    -- and binds only once the handshake has completed, consuming the token
    that completion produced.                                               */
rule Bind_After_Handshake:
    [ Handshaken($N, $C), !EarlyData($C, a) ]
  --[ Bound($N, $C, a), BoundAfterHandshake($N, $C, a) ]->
    [ ]

/*  AND THE SERVER THAT PROCESSES EARLY DATA.  No handshake token consumed,
    so nothing limits how often it fires -- which is the re-binding the rule
    exists to prevent.                                                      */
rule Bind_From_Early_Data:
    [ !EarlyData($C, a) ]
  --[ Bound($N, $C, a), ProcessedEarlyData($N) ]->
    [ ]

/*  THE OBLIGATION.  On a server that defers early data, every binding
    follows a completed handshake.                                          */
lemma a_conforming_server_binds_only_after_the_handshake:
  all-traces
  "All N C a #i.
      Bound(N, C, a) @ #i
      & not(Ex X #s. ProcessedEarlyData(X) @ #s)
        ==> Ex #h. HandshakeDone(N, C) @ #h & #h < #i"

/*  ANTI-VACUITY: a conforming server does bind.                            */
lemma a_conforming_binding_is_reachable:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C a #i.
      BoundAfterHandshake(N, C, a) @ #i
    & not(Ex X #s. ProcessedEarlyData(X) @ #s)"

/*  THE HARM, KEPT REACHABLE.  A server that acts on early data binds the
    same `Attach` twice off one replayable message and with no second
    handshake -- "a replayed `Attach` re-binds session state".  If this ever
    stops being reachable the model has stopped representing replayability
    and the obligation above would hold for the wrong reason.               */
lemma a_replayed_attach_rebinds_on_a_server_that_processes_early_data:
  exists-trace
  "Ex N C a #i #j.
      Bound(N, C, a) @ #i & Bound(N, C, a) @ #j & not(#i = #j)
    & ProcessedEarlyData(N) @ #i"


end
